<https://globular.io/awareness#forbiddenFix/forbidden_fix.suppress_doctor_findings_when_desired_state_is_empty_or_unre> <https://globular.io/awareness#authoredIn> "docs/awareness/forbidden_fixes.yaml" .
<https://globular.io/awareness#forbiddenFix/forbidden_fix.treat_a_certificate_file_s_existence_as_proof_it_is_usable> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://globular.io/awareness#ForbiddenFix> .
<https://globular.io/awareness#forbiddenFix/forbidden_fix.treat_a_certificate_file_s_existence_as_proof_it_is_usable> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Gating certificate work on presence rather than validity: waitForFiles/os.Stat to decide a cert is fine, GenerateSignedServerCertificate returning early because server.crt exists, or reusing a leaf because the CA fingerprint is unchanged. A certificate can be present and expired, present and key-mismatched, present and missing a required SAN, or present and signed by a retired CA. Every one of those reads as OK to an existence check, and the result is a system that reports 'repaired runtime TLS certificate' over material it just found invalid. Gate on NeedsCertRegeneration (presence, key/cert coherence, chain verify, expiry, renew-before window, SANs) and re-validate after any repair before claiming success." .
<https://globular.io/awareness#forbiddenFix/forbidden_fix.treat_a_certificate_file_s_existence_as_proof_it_is_usable> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Treat a certificate file's existence as proof it is usable" .
<https://globular.io/awareness#forbiddenFix/forbidden_fix.treat_a_certificate_file_s_existence_as_proof_it_is_usable> <https://globular.io/awareness#affects> <https://globular.io/awareness#invariant/diagnostics.must_measure_reality> .
<https://globular.io/awareness#forbiddenFix/forbidden_fix.treat_a_certificate_file_s_existence_as_proof_it_is_usable> <https://globular.io/awareness#authoredIn> "docs/awareness/forbidden_fixes.yaml" .
<https://globular.io/awareness#forbiddenFix/forbidden_fix.treat_a_non_empty_response_body_as_proof_a_certificate_was_i> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://globular.io/awareness#ForbiddenFix> .
<https://globular.io/awareness#forbiddenFix/forbidden_fix.treat_a_non_empty_response_body_as_proof_a_certificate_was_i> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Accepting the output of a signing request because the file is non-empty, or because the HTTP status was 2xx, without parsing it as a certificate and confirming its public key matches the private key the node just generated. A redirect body, an error page, or a mis-issued certificate all satisfy 'non-empty' and then fail much later as an unexplained TLS handshake error, far from the cause. Also forbidden: leaving the private key on disk when signing fails — a key with no certificate is what makes node-agent restart-loop on 'cert not ready' instead of the join failing visibly at the point of failure." .
<https://globular.io/awareness#forbiddenFix/forbidden_fix.treat_a_non_empty_response_body_as_proof_a_certificate_was_i> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Treat a non-empty response body as proof a certificate was issued" .
<https://globular.io/awareness#forbiddenFix/forbidden_fix.treat_a_non_empty_response_body_as_proof_a_certificate_was_i> <https://globular.io/awareness#affects> <https://globular.io/awareness#invariant/meta.half_done_must_not_look_done> .
<https://globular.io/awareness#forbiddenFix/forbidden_fix.treat_a_non_empty_response_body_as_proof_a_certificate_was_i> <https://globular.io/awareness#authoredIn> "docs/awareness/forbidden_fixes.yaml" .
